============================================================================
HIERARCHY: THE DENSITY OF STATES, k_F, AND THE BEND-OVER
============================================================================

[1] Does k_F actually enter the gap condition?
    Assemble lambda = N_0 * <V>_FS from 6c's own pieces, keeping k_F symbolic.

      N_0      = k_F^2 / (pi^2 v)                  [one band, both spins]
      e^2      = 4 pi alpha_c                      [Heaviside-Lorentz]
      V(q)     = e^2 / (q^2 + m_D^2)
      u        = q^2/(4 k_F^2),  so <.>_FS = int_0^1 du
      m_D^2    = e^2 * 2 N_0                       [2-for-screening]

      b  = m_D^2/(4 k_F^2) = 4 pi alpha_c * 2 k_F^2/(pi^2 v) / (4 k_F^2)
         = 2 alpha_c/(pi v)                        <-- k_F CANCELS

      <V>_FS = int_0^1 du  e^2/(4 k_F^2 u + m_D^2)
             = (e^2/4k_F^2) int_0^1 du/(u+b)
             = (e^2/4k_F^2) ln(1 + 1/b)            <-- carries 1/k_F^2

      lambda = N_0 <V>_FS = (k_F^2/pi^2 v)(e^2/4k_F^2) ln(1+1/b)
             = (alpha_c/(pi v)) ln(1 + 1/b)        <-- k_F CANCELS AGAIN

    Numerical check, k_F swept over eight decades:

      k_F            lambda
      1e-4         0.029874162784
      1e-3         0.029874162784
      1e-2         0.029874162784
      1e-1         0.029874162784
      1e+0         0.029874162784
      1e+1         0.029874162784
      1e+2         0.029874162784
      1e+3         0.029874162784
      1e+4         0.029874162784

    spread across eight decades: 1.041e-17

    k_F does not appear in the answer. It cancels twice -- once out of the
    screening constant b, and once between N_0 and the Fermi-surface average.
    The docket asks for a number that the construction does not contain.

[2] What the construction does require of k_F
    Not a value -- an inequality. Three conditions, all of them one-sided:

      (i)   k_F > 0 strictly. At k_F = 0 the Fermi surface degenerates to the
            node, N_0 -> 0, and 6a's own table records the consequence: J is
            convergent, there is no log, and pairing needs lambda >= 2/3 rather
            than 0.03. The node cannot pair. This is the condition that matters.
      (ii)  E_F = v k_F below the bend-over, so the shell sits inside the linear
            cone where rho ~ E^2 and the DOS formula used is the right one.
      (iii) the shell locally flat, i.e. Delta << E_F, the usual BCS assumption.

    So the honest statement is that the hierarchy construction is INDEPENDENT of
    where the Fermi surface sits, provided it exists and lies inside the cone.
    That is a stronger result than a fitted k_F would have been: a value would
    have been one more thing to justify, and there is nothing to justify.

[3] Recomputing k rather than adopting it

    b        = 2 alpha_c/pi      = 0.0139369168   (corpus: 0.0139369)
    k        = ln(1+1/b)/pi     = 1.36461191   (corpus: 1.36461191)
    lambda   = k * alpha_c      = 0.02987416
    d lnM/d lnk = 1/(k alpha_c) = 33.47   (corpus: 33.47)

    counterfactual, one-band screening: k = 1.58305 (corpus: 1.58305)
    The 2-for-screening is load-bearing, exactly as 6c says: dropping it moves k
    by 16% and the anchor by two orders through the 33x amplification.

[4] The bend-over spectrum: does it condense at lambda = 0.03?
    Spectrum: rho ~ E^p below E_*, flat above. 6a records the shell integral as
    J = 1/p + ln(Lambda/E_*), verified there at p = 1,2,3. Re-verify from the
    integral itself rather than trusting the closed form:

      p = 1:  numeric 5.605170   closed form 5.605170   diff 2.32e-12
      p = 2:  numeric 5.105173   closed form 5.105170   diff 2.50e-06
      p = 3:  numeric 4.938506   closed form 4.938504   diff 2.50e-06

    Gap condition 1 = lambda * J  =>  ln(Lambda/E_*) = 1/lambda - 1/p.
      p = 1:  ln(Lambda/E_*) =   32.474  =>  Lambda/E_* = e^32.47
      p = 2:  ln(Lambda/E_*) =   32.974  =>  Lambda/E_* = e^32.97
      p = 3:  ln(Lambda/E_*) =   33.140  =>  Lambda/E_* = e^33.14

    A solution EXISTS for every p: the condition is satisfiable, so the
    bend-over spectrum does condense at lambda = 0.03. That is the half of the
    docket that asks whether it condenses, and the answer is yes.

[5] Why 'it condenses' is not the same as 'it delivers the anchor'
    The condition fixes Lambda/E_* -- the ratio of the cutoff to the BEND-OVER,
    not a gap. Delta has dropped out of the equation entirely. So what the
    bend-over spectrum determines is where the node emerges, not the scale the
    hierarchy needs, and 6a already records the consequence: at p = 2 it lands
    11.65 TeV against the anchor's 1576 GeV, over by e^2 = 7.4x, AND with the
    wrong sign -- a deficit that raises the scale where suppression is needed.
    That route is in the failures ledger and this script does not revive it.

    The two results are compatible and both are needed: the spectrum condenses
    (this file), and the condensate it forms is not what sets the anchor (ledger).

============================================================================
VERDICT on #58
============================================================================

    The density of states was already supplied: N_0 = k_F^2/(pi^2 v), 6a.

    k_F cannot be supplied because the construction does not contain it. It cancels
    twice -- out of the screening constant b, and between N_0 and the Fermi-surface
    average -- leaving lambda = (alpha_c/pi v) ln(1 + 1/b) with no trace of it.
    Verified numerically across eight decades of k_F to 1e-16.

    What the construction requires instead is an inequality: k_F > 0 strictly, sitting
    inside the linear cone. The strictness is the whole content -- at k_F = 0 the DOS
    vanishes, there is no log, and pairing would need lambda >= 2/3 instead of 0.03.

    The bend-over spectrum DOES condense at lambda = 0.03: the gap condition
    ln(Lambda/E_*) = 1/lambda - 1/p has a solution for every p. But it fixes the
    node-emergence scale rather than a gap, and the ledger already records that this
    route misses the anchor by e^2 with the wrong sign. Condensing and delivering the
    anchor are different claims; only the first is established here.

    Net: the docket's first half is answered by showing the quantity does not exist,
    which is a cleaner outcome than a value would have been -- a fitted k_F would have
    been one more number needing justification. The second half is answered yes, with
    the standing caveat that it does not source the exponent.

